1. A solar-powered water purification device comprising:  
   a photovoltaic panel configured to convert solar radiation into electrical energy;  
   a fluid inlet for receiving untreated water from an external source;  
   an electrically driven pump powered by the photovoltaic panel for pressurizing the untreated water;  
   a multi-stage filtration unit in fluid communication with the pump, the filtration unit comprising at least a sediment filter and an activated carbon filter for removing particulates and organic contaminants;  
   a UV disinfection chamber downstream of the multi-stage filtration unit, the chamber housing at least one UV-C light source configured to inactivate microbial pathogens; and  
   an electronic controller electrically coupled to the photovoltaic panel, the pump, and the UV-C light source, the controller being configured to distribute power from the photovoltaic panel to the pump and the UV-C light source;  
   wherein the device is configured to produce potable water from contaminated sources using only solar-derived energy, thereby providing a portable off-grid water purification solution for remote or disaster-stricken areas lacking reliable electrical infrastructure.  

2. The device of claim 1, wherein the multi-stage filtration unit comprises a replaceable cartridge assembly including:  
   a first stage sediment filter having a pore size in the range of 1 to 10 microns; and  
   a second stage activated carbon block filter impregnated with silver nanoparticles to enhance antimicrobial adsorption.  

3. The device of claims 1 and 2, wherein the photovoltaic panel comprises a foldable monocrystalline solar array with an integrated maximum power point tracking (MPPT) module, further comprising:  
   a rechargeable battery module electrically connected to the controller;  
   wherein the controller is further configured to regulate charging and discharging of the battery module and to automatically switch power supply between the photovoltaic panel and the battery module to maintain continuous operation of the pump and UV-C light source during periods of variable solar irradiation.
